This file shows the expected shape of a cert in this directory. It is NOT a real
certificate and the install step ignores it — the glob only matches *.crt, and this
file ends in .example.

Copy your real root CA here as <name>.crt. The contents must look exactly like this:
a single PEM block, base64 between the BEGIN/END markers, nothing else.

-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----
MIIE6DCCA9CgAwIBAgIJANu+mC2Jt3uTMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBCwUAMIGhMQswCQYD
... roughly 20-40 more lines of base64 ...
QSBSb290IENBMB4XDTE0MTIwODE5NDMwNFoXDTQyMTIwMTE5NDMwNFowgaExCzAJ
-----END CERTIFICATE-----

If your proxy chain needs more than one certificate, put each one in its own .crt
file rather than concatenating them into one. update-ca-certificates indexes each
staged file by its own hash for directory-style (CApath) lookups; a concatenated
file only gets indexed under one hash, so tools that verify via CApath instead of
the combined CAfile bundle can fail to discover the other certs in it.

Common mistakes this format check catches:

  Binary / DER      file starts with random bytes, not "-----BEGIN"
                    fix: openssl x509 -inform der -in in.cer -out out.crt

  PKCS#7 / .p7b     starts with "-----BEGIN PKCS7-----"
                    fix: openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -in in.p7b -out out.crt

  Wrong cert        it parses, but "Basic Constraints" has no CA:TRUE — you exported
                    the server leaf instead of the root
